@startuml client_connection_send_with_callback_activity
title ClientConnection - sending with a reply callback

start
:SendWithCallback(message, callback);
if (message.size() > max_send_size_?) then (yes)
    :Return EMSGSIZE;
    stop
else (no)
    :Message size is admissible;
endif
if (state_ != kReady?) then (yes)
    :Return EINVAL;
    stop
else (no)
    :Connection is ready at the admission check;
endif

:Lock send_mutex_;
if (truly_async or waiting_for_reply_ is engaged?) then (yes)
    :TryQueueMessage(message, move(callback));
    if (Message admitted to send_queue_?) then (yes)
        :Admission handles guarded queue arming;
    else (no)
        :Unlock send_mutex_;
        :Return ENOBUFS: no free send command;
        stop
    endif
else (no)
    :SendProtocolMessage(client_fd_, REQUEST, message) under send_mutex_;
    if (Transport failed?) then (yes)
        :Unlock send_mutex_;
        :Return the transport error;
        stop
    else (no)
        :Move callback into waiting_for_reply_;
    endif
endif

:Unlock send_mutex_;
:Return local acceptance;
stop

legend
    fully_ordered does not select request admission behavior.
    An engaged slot may be a pending reply or a queue-draining marker.
    Direct transport runs before installing this reply callback,
    while send_mutex_ protects the slot from concurrent reply processing.
    Local rejection or direct-send failure does not invoke the callback.
    After acceptance, reply/error delivery is a separate continuation.
endlegend

@enduml